 {'sec': 'P3.2', 'title': 'Generators: functions that pause', 'beats': [{'say': 'How do you process a 50 gigabyte file on a laptop with 8 gigabytes of memory? You never hold more than one record at a time. Generators make that easy.', 'show': {'t': 'big', 'text': '50 GB file, 8 GB laptop', 'sub': 'one record at a time'}}, {'say': 'A function with yield does not run when you call it. It returns a generator. Each time the loop asks for the next value, it runs until the next yield, hands back one value, and pauses right there, keeping its local variables.', 'show': {'t': 'code', 'text': 'def read_trips(path):\n    with open(path) as f:\n        for line in f:\n            yield parse(line)   # pause here'}}, {'say': 'So memory stays flat whatever the file size. The catch: a generator is used once. Loop over it a second time and you get nothing.', 'show': {'t': 'points', 'items': ['Lazy: one value at a time', 'Memory stays flat', 'Used once: second loop is empty']}}], 'check': {'q': 'You loop over the same generator twice. What does the second loop see?', 'opts': ['The same values again', 'Nothing', 'An error'], 'ans': 1, 'why': 'A generator is exhausted after one pass; make a new one or store a list if it is small.'}, 'work': ['Read big inputs with generators that yield parsed records', 'Never pass a generator to two consumers; create one per pass']},
